{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact S1 source-order/source-time child 684002a4b760d7f1fbd90b960e3d9288e572b3ea, tree e492aec7389ed9d2eabb219393a50b00262afb37, against Main 165d5a86fe8c1868f3069efad61a435a5417bde2/tree 1b69aa4787f8a64fca1bc299567c886527bdc27f. I read primary 9233a8c1 FIRST and all 18 exact artifacts. All 2254 Git leaves were materialized and checked by mode/blob; 2236 prior leaves are unchanged. This closes no late-form or sequenced implementation review.\n\nF1 (P1), packages/derive/src/delivery.ts, the new source-order early return: preserve the native settlement obligation of a suppressed reserved request, or reject the incompatible declaration. The validator currently accepts order:source on the native export-license tell handler. reservedFor has already identified a standing receiver-pin and its numbered decision before the new guard, but decide(\"superseded\", undefined) defaults to settles:false and no native record effects. Unlike the existing refusal helper, it does not raise that pin's decided counter. The receiver seals a terminal result while retaining the reservation for the very decision just recorded. At a capacity boundary it also tries to fund this as new work rather than the entry already reserved for it.\n\nMy paired counterexample uses the actual holdCapability, validateDefinition, judgeDelivery and MemoryState, with the existing fictional receiver-pin fixture extended to two exports. Both exports have native standing pins at k=1 for one task source. The later source entry 101 licenses export B at k=2 and applies; entry 100 then licenses export A at k=2. Source order seals A as superseded, settles:false, leaves A.decided=1 and reserves 3 remaining entries across the pins. The arrival-order control applies A, leaves A.decided=2 and reserves 2. No invalid field, failing handler guard or forged delivery digest explains the difference. The task source entries and per-key authority are explicitly scripted fixture inputs